WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.510 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  god  thou hast allowed me to live till now i  thank thee and offer up my sufferings to  thee  and pray thee at the same time to take  me further into my protection  this and several other pious meditations  i had with god  and i considered my destiny although it  was never quiet the entire night and  though a new battle might have started  at any hour  none of all my miseries were so hard and  depressing  as the thought of my brothers sisters  and friends this thought was my greatest  pain  which i sought to repress with this hope  with god  everything is possible so i will depend  upon  his further help  in the month of january 1812 i was  recalled to the garrison of schondorf  from here the entire corpse marched  through the wurzburg district  where it was generally rumored that we  were going to spain and would embark  onto the baltic sea  although the outlook did not seem good i  and all the other soldiers were very  merry  always singing and dancing especially  since throughout the entire verzeberg  country  the quarters and eating and drinking  were very good particularly because of  the large supply of wine  so that everyone voluntarily had his  flask filled with wine and his pockets  with cookies  at the time of departure moreover the  beautiful villages on the main river  surrounded by vineyards fruit trees and  grain fields  put everyone in a happy mood we were  still very lively in these towns  singing and laughing cheerfully although  we could imagine  the unusual campaign before us but  everyone always believes in and  hopes for the best  this video is sponsored by the great  courses plus the premiere online  learning service  the great courses plus have been amazing  supporters of the work of me and my  brother for nearly three years now  and i've been using them non-stop during  that time it's an  amazing resource and if i need to brush  up my knowledge on any topic  no matter how huge they are who i turn  to  recently i have devoured their  foundation of western and eastern  civilization  lecture series incredibly detailed  explorations of pretty much everything  that modern society stands upon  from the first cities to the 20th  century fascinating  university level stuff ordered a very  reasonable subscription for what is  essentially a mounted of easily  digestible knowledge  i honestly can't recommend it highly  enough so check them out at  thegreatcoursesplus.com forward slash  voices  to get a free trial and show your  support for the channel too  i started with a free trial and never  looked back  [Music]  thanks  [Music]  from there the line of the march turned  towards frankfurt on the oda  where a halt was made here we were  quartered for three days and  by this time we had to be contented with  poor food and  regiment bread we had to drill even on  ascension day  so general hugo tried to remind his  royal highness the crown prince and not  to drill  saying that it was a holiday the crown  prince however  gave this answer i will do you a favour  general  and not arrest you do you think i don't  know what day it is  on corpus christi day we marched into  the city of torn  here for the first time we saw all the  corpse streaming together  all the gates were jammed and the  regiments had to wind through the  streets in a great throng  we still obtained quarters however we  had to prepare our own  food from our ration meat and bread the  meat came from the salted ice pits  there was a rumor that it had been  stored from the war of 1807.  the condition of the meat made the rumor  seem credible since the meat appeared  bluish black and was sharp as herrings  daily the hardships increased and there  was no hope of bread  my colonel spoke to us once and said  that we could hope for no more bread  until we crossed the enemy border  on june 25th the army went over the  bridges  we now believe that once in russia we  need do nothing but forage which  however proved to be an illusion  [Applause]  the towns were already stripped before  we could enter and so were all the  villagers  here and there a hog ran around and was  beaten with clubs chopped with sabers  and stabbed with bayonets  several times i succeeded in cutting off  something but i had to chew it and eat  it  uncooked since my hunger could not wait  for a chance to boil the meat  during the third night a halt was made  in a field which was trampled  into a swamp here we were ordered to  camp and to make fires  since neither village nor forest could  be seen and the rain  continued without end well you can  imagine in  what a half numbed condition everyone  stood here what could we do  there was nothing that we could do but  stack the rifles in pyramids  and keep moving in order not to freeze  meanwhile a cellar full of brandy had  been discovered i too pushed myself into  the cellar  and filled my field flask i returned to  the shelter with this  and drank it without even any bread then  by noon  i noticed that half the men had stayed  back and several  had suffocated in the swamp the brandy  helped  but many a man drank himself to death  because he would become numbed  and would freeze on account of the wet  and cold  it then happened to be good weather but  in order to obtain  water for drinking and cooking holes  were dug into the swamps three feet deep  in which the water collected the water  was  very warm however and was reddish brown  with millions of little red worms  so that it had to be bound in linen and  sucked through with the mouth  this was of course a hardship for any  man  and particularly for us living as we  were  the march was kept up day and night  one man after another stretched himself  half dead upon the ground most of them  died a few hours later  several however suddenly fell to the  ground  dead the chief cause of this was thirst  for in most districts there was no water  fit for drinking  so the men had to drink out of the  ditches in which were dead horses  and dead men  on august 16 we came towards the city of  smolensk  here my company had only 25  fit men on the morning of august 17th  every regiment was set in motion and all  advanced in columns against the russians  here every regiment without exception  was under fire again  and again the troops attempted assaults  but because of the greater number of  russians  we were forced back every time on this  day  since their heavy artillery stood on the  heights and could hit everything  finally by night we had made good our  position on the heights overlooking the  city  and the battle was discontinued  that night the thought of the coming day  alternated with fitful sleep  and in fantasy the many dead men and  horses came as a world of spirits  before the last judgment  as soon as the day broke we marched  against the city  the river was crossed below the city the  suburbs on the northern side were  stormed  set on fire and burnt up my company's  doctor  named stauble had his arm shot away in  crossing the stream  and he died afterwards no longer could i  pay any attention to my comrades and  therefore  knew not in what way they perished or  were lost  everyone fired and struck at the enemy  in wild madness and no one could tell  whether he was in front in the middle or  behind the center of the army  on august the 19th the entire army moved  forward and pursued the russians with  all speed  from smelensk to mashaysk the war  displayed its horrible work of  destruction  all the roads fields and woods lay as  though sown with people  horses wagons burned villages and cities  everything looked like the complete ruin  of all that lived  in particular we saw ten dead russians  to one of our men although every day our  numbers fell off considerably  the cities in the meantime were dora  gaboush semlevo  viasma and gashatsk the march up to  there  as far as it was a march is  indescribable and  inconceivable for people who have not  seen anything of it  the very great heat the dust which was  like a thick fog  the closed line of marching columns and  the putrid water from holes  filled with dead people and cattle  brought everyone  close to death and eye pains fatigue  thirst and hunger  tormented everybody god how i remembered  the bread and beer which i'd enjoyed at  home with such indifferent pleasure  how gladly would i renounce my whole  life the warm  food so common at home if i only did not  lack  good bread and beer now wherever i  looked i saw the soldiers with dead  half-desperate faces  many cried out in despair if only my  mother had not borne me  some demoralized men even cursed their  parents  and their birth  on the route to moscow on september the  7th every corpse was assigned its place  and the signal to attack was given  [Music]  like thunderbolts the firing began both  against and from the enemy  this beautiful grain region without  woods and villages  could now be compared to a cleared  forest  a few trunks here and there looking grey  and white  within a space an hour and a half long  and wide the ground  was covered with people and animals we  moved forward and camped by a forest on  a height facing moscow  it was full of green trees now we  traveled with somewhat higher hopes  towards moscow  yet with the expectation that we should  clash again with the russians  but the russians thought themselves too  weak  and went through the city setting fire  to many parts  and the inhabitants were abandoned  on the march into the city or rather on  the march towards it  from a hill in a forest an hour and a  half away we saw  the huge city lying before us  clouds of fire red smoke great gilded  crosses of the church towers glittered  shimmered and billowed up towards us  from the city  this holy city was like the description  of the city of jerusalem  over which our savior wept it even  resembled the horror  and the wasting according to the gospel  [Music]  after a few hours walking we went past  the palace kremlin  in moscow one could find and buy  provisions for each soldier was now  citizen merchant innkeeper and baker of  moscow  whoever could find nothing could buy  something and vegetables in sufficient  quantity stood in the fields it was  still good weather and one could sleep  warm enough  under a coat at night after we had been  citizens of moscow for four weeks  we lost our burger rights again napoleon  refused the peace treaty proposed to him  and the army which had advanced some 30  hours further on  had to retreat because the russian army  stationed in moldavia was approaching  now was october the 17th and napoleon  held an army review  and announced the departure for october  18th early in the morning at 3 o'clock  with the warning that whoever should  delay one hour would fall  into the hands of the enemies all beer  brandy etc was abandoned  and whatever was still intact was  ordered to be burnt napoleon himself had  the kremlin undermined  and blown up then everyone packed up and  the enemy  attacked us the decision was soon to the  advantage of the russians  and all ran in a crowded retreat the  army moving towards heluga  with the cossacks in front and behind us  those who were too weak to carry their  weapons or knapsacks  threw them away and all looked like a  crowd of gypsies  the fighting the shrieking the firing of  large and small guns hunger and thirst  and all conceivable  torments increased the never-ending  confusion  indeed even the lice seemed to seek  supremacy  for their number on both officers and  privates was in the thousands  in these days it snowed for the first  time and the snow  remained the cold arrived at the same  time too  and the freezing of the people  multiplied the number of the dead  no one could walk 50 paces without  seeing men stretched out half  or completely dead we arrived at  smolensk on november 12th having made  from moscow to that city  26 days and nights of travel without  pausing a day  here we settled down and had to camp for  two days  every night the fires for warming could  be seen over a region  four hours long and wide reddening the  sky like red cloth  again and again people died and  sometimes froze to death  these were people who pressed towards  the fire but were seldom permitted to  get there  so they died away from the fire and very  often  they were even converted into cushions  in order that the living would not have  to sit  on the snow  when we came near the beresina river  there was a place where napoleon ordered  the pack horses to be unharnessed  and where he ate he watched his army  pass by in the most  wretched condition what he may have felt  in his heart is impossible to surmise  his outward appearance seemed  indifferent and unconcerned over the  wretchedness of his soldiers  only ambition and lost honor may have  made themselves felt in his heart  in this region we came to a half-burned  village away from the road  in which a cellar was found under a  mansion we sought for potatoes and  and i also pressed down the broad  stairway although the cellar was already  half filled with people  when i was at the bottom of the steps  the screaming began  under my feet everyone crowded in and no  one could get out  here people were trampled to death and  suffocated those who wanted to stoop  down for something were bowled over by  those standing  and had to be stepped upon in spite of  the murderous shrieking and frightful  groaning the pressure from outside  increased  the poor deathly weak men who fell had  to lie there  until dead under the feet of their own  comrades  i thought of my friends at home and  compared my misery and approaching end  with my former life of plenty  i remember the common saying at home a  campaign is always made out to be worse  than it was  with this common notion i consoled  myself thinking  it's well that you my beloved kindred  and friends  know nothing of my condition for it  would only cause you pain and it would  be of no use  to me i gave up pushing into the cellar  and i thought in cold fear how will i  get out again  i pressed flat against the wall so it  afforded me shelter and pushed myself  vigorously little by little up the  stairs  this was almost impossible with others  treading on my long coat  finally i came to ordersburg and for the  first time was given regular  quarters from this city i went on to  moava  and at that place received quarters  again it was just christmas eve  a date i would not have known if i had  not learnt it from the landlord  the washing of my hands and face  proceeded very slowly  because the crusts on my hands ears and  nose had grown like  fur lark with cracks and cold black  scales  i washed then for an hour with hot water  and soap however i felt i had only  become  somewhat smoother and lighter but i  could not notice any removal of the  blackness  and the scales  finally on february the 24th 1813  i arrived at elvangan my convoy to here  had left behind 100 dead out of 175  men all at once i saw my brother-in-law  and my brother  they would not have recognized me of  course as i looked then but i cooled  thrust out my hand  and greeted them they jumped into the  air for joy and pressed my hands  and our hearts alone could feel for we  could not speak  oh that all people might know how high  the love of friends and relatives can  mount through such a meeting  one feels in it heavenly joy the  all-wise providence of god  and at the same time the miracle of  nature  [Music]

